/ Coxeter groups → every regular & semiregular polyhedron, one recipe
/ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/ A Coxeter group is generated by reflections in a handful of mirrors
/ that meet at angles π/m.  In 3D a *linear* diagram is all it takes —
/ three mirrors, described by just two integers p,q:
/
/            o───p───o───q───o           mirror1 ∠ mirror2 = π/p
/         mirror1  mirror2  mirror3       mirror2 ∠ mirror3 = π/q
/                                         mirror1 ∠ mirror3 = π/2
/
/ Changing those two integers changes the symmetry of space itself:
/
/     [3,3] → A₃   order 24    tetrahedral   (rotations A₄)
/     [4,3] → B₃   order 48    octahedral    (rotations S₄)
/     [5,3] → H₃   order 120   icosahedral   (rotations A₅)
/
/ Wythoff's construction then turns a group into a SOLID: drop a seed
/ point into the fundamental triangle bounded by the three mirrors and
/ reflect it everywhere.  The seed's position — three "ring weights"
/ (t₀ t₁ t₂), how far it sits from each mirror — selects the polyhedron:
/
/     1 0 0  regular {p,q}         0 1 0  rectified
/     0 0 1  dual    {q,p}         1 1 0  truncated {p,q}
/     1 0 1  cantellated           0 1 1  truncated {q,p}
/     1 1 1  omnitruncated  (a seed off every mirror → orbit = |G|)
/
/ So (p, q; t₀ t₁ t₂) is a five-integer ADDRESS for a uniform polyhedron,
/ and this ONE method reaches every Platonic solid AND every Archimedean
/ solid in these families.  The golden-ratio coordinates and ±1 cubes of
/ the other constructions are just special seeds of this kaleidoscope.
/
/ Run: ink demo/geometry.k

PI: 3.14159265

/ ── vector / matrix helpers ───────────────────────────────────────────
/ NB: the prelude math names (sqrt nrm …) are NOUNS — an op-glyph right
/ after one is dyadic, so `sqrt +/x*x` parses as a seeded fold.  Always
/ bracket: sqrt[…], nrm[…]  (see AGENT.md "seeded-fold" trap).
sqr:   {x*x}
rnd:   {(_ 0.5+x*1e3)%1e3}              / snap to 1e-3 grid → float-safe ~ match
nrm:   {x%sqrt[+/x*x]}                  / unit vector
cross: {((x[1]*y 2)-x[2]*y 1;(x[2]*y 0)-x[0]*y 2;(x[0]*y 1)-x[1]*y 0)}
mm:    {x {+/x*y}/:\: +y}               / 3x3 · 3x3
id3:   (1. 0. 0.;0. 1. 0.;0. 0. 1.)
refl:  {y-(2*+/x*y)*x}                  / reflect point y in the mirror with unit normal x
rmat:  {id3-2*x*\:x}                    / the same reflection, as a matrix I - 2nnᵀ
det:   {+/x[0]*cross[x 1;x 2]}          / +1 for a rotation, -1 for a reflection

/ ── §1  the Coxeter diagram → three mirror normals ────────────────────
/ Unit normals with n₁·n₂ = -cos(π/p), n₂·n₃ = -cos(π/q), n₁·n₃ = 0.
mirrors: {[p;q]
  a:cos PI%p;s:sin PI%p;b:(-cos PI%q)%s
  ((1. 0. 0.); ((-a);s;0.); (0.;b;sqrt[1.-b*b]))}

/ Corner directions of the fundamental triangle (the reciprocal basis):
/ corner i is fixed by the *other* two mirrors — where the seed sits when
/ only ring i is lit.  A weighted sum lands the seed anywhere inside.
corners: {[N] (cross[N 1;N 2]; cross[N 2;N 0]; cross[N 0;N 1])}

/ ── §2  one breadth-first closure, used twice ────────────────────────
/ The symmetry group and the Wythoff orbit are the SAME algorithm: from
/ a seed, keep applying a fixed set of maps to the frontier until nothing
/ new appears.  Only two things vary between them — how the frontier is
/ EXPANDED, and how deep values are rounded for the dedup key — so both
/ are passed in and carried through the loop state (lambdas don't capture
/ parent scope, k idiom — so the expander/key travel as data).  Values
/ stay EXACT throughout; rounding is only the key — rounding the elements
/ feeds error back through the maps and the closure never settles.
/ The loop state is a `search`: a dict bundling the expansion rule, the
/ dedup key, the `seen` (explored) set and the `frontier` still to grow —
/ the textbook quadruple, the way a `particle` bundles position+velocity.
/ `expand` and `key` ride along unchanged; `seen` accumulates, `frontier`
/ is the newly found elements to grow next round.
bfc.step: {[search]
  expand:   search`expand   / frontier → candidate elements
  key:      search`key      / dedup normaliser (how deep to round)
  seen:     search`seen     / everything found so far (explored set)
  frontier: search`frontier / the newest elements, still to grow
  cand: expand frontier
  ra:   key seen
  rc:   key cand
  k1:   ?rc
  k2:   k1@&(#ra)=ra?k1                  / keys not seen before
  nw:   cand@rc?k2                       / one exact representative each
  [expand:expand; key:key; seen:seen,nw; frontier:nw]}
bfc: {[expand;key;seed]                  / breadth-first closure
  search: [expand:expand; key:key; seen:,seed; frontier:,seed]
  ({0<#x`frontier}bfc.step/search)`seen}

/ §2a  the reflection group: expand = frontier · the 3 generator matrices.
/ group.expand[gens] is a PARTIAL carrying its generators, so `bfc` needs
/ no special-casing — just a different expander and a deeper round (rnd'').
group.expand: {[G;fr] ,/ fr {mm[x;y]}/:\: G}
group:        {[N] bfc[group.expand[rmat' N]; rnd''; id3]}

/ ── §3  Wythoff's construction: reflect the seed in every mirror ──────
/ Same closure, one-level round; orbit.expand[N] carries the mirror set.
orbit.expand: {[N;fr] ,/ N {refl[x;y]}/:\: fr}
orbit:        {[N;seed] rnd' bfc[orbit.expand[N]; rnd'; seed]}
wythoff:      {[p;q;t] N:mirrors[p;q]; orbit[N; nrm[+/ t*corners N]]}

/ ── §4  the 1-skeleton, from nothing but distance ────────────────────
/ Vertices come from the orbit; an edge is any vertex pair at the
/ MINIMUM nonzero distance; faces follow from Euler V-E+F = 2.  This is
/ Steinitz's viewpoint — the graph alone fixes the polyhedron.
adj:   {d: x {+/sqr x-y}/:\: x; m: &/,/{x@&x>1e-6}'d; 1e-3>sqr d-m}   / vertices → boolean adjacency
edges: {&,/ x&(!#x)<\:!#x}       / adjacency → count of pairs i<j that touch

/ ── run: group orders + orbit–stabiliser ─────────────────────────────
`0 0: "reflection groups from the diagram [p,q]:"
grp: {[nm;p;q]
  G: group mirrors[p;q]
  `0 0: nm,"  |G|=",($#G),"  rotations=",$+/0<{det x}'G}
grp["A3 [3,3] tetrahedral";3;3]
grp["B3 [4,3] octahedral ";4;3]
grp["H3 [5,3] icosahedral";5;3]
`0 0: ""
`0 0: "orbit-stabiliser  |orbit| = |G| / |stab|  (H3, |G|=120):"
`0 0: "  seed on 2 mirrors 1 0 0 → icosa-face stab 6 → ",($#wythoff[5;3;1. 0. 0.])," verts"
`0 0: "  seed on 2 mirrors 0 0 1 → icosa-vert stab 10 → ",($#wythoff[5;3;0. 0. 1.])," verts"
`0 0: "  seed on 1 mirror  0 1 0 → edge stab 4 → ",($#wythoff[5;3;0. 1. 0.])," verts"
`0 0: "  seed off all      1 1 1 → trivial stab 1 → ",($#wythoff[5;3;1. 1. 1.])," verts = |G|"

/ ── census: name, (p,q,ring), V, E, F, Euler ─────────────────────────
census: {[nm;p;q;t]
  V: wythoff[p;q;t]
  e: # edges adj V
  f: 2+e-#V
  ring: ,/$_ t
  `0 0: nm," [",($p),",",($q),"] ",ring,"  V=",($#V)," E=",($e)," F=",($f)," χ=",$((#V)+f)-e}

`0 0: ""
`0 0: "the five Platonic solids — pure ring choices:"
census["tetrahedron   ";3;3;1. 0. 0.]
census["cube          ";4;3;1. 0. 0.]
census["octahedron    ";4;3;0. 0. 1.]
census["dodecahedron  ";5;3;1. 0. 0.]
census["icosahedron   ";5;3;0. 0. 1.]

`0 0: ""
`0 0: "…and 'other shapes': rectify or truncate the SAME mirrors →"
`0 0: "the Archimedean solids, no new geometry, just a different seed:"
census["cuboctahedron ";4;3;0. 1. 0.]       / rectified cube
census["truncated cube";4;3;1. 1. 0.]
census["trunc. octahed";4;3;0. 1. 1.]
census["rhombicubocta ";4;3;1. 0. 1.]
census["trunc.cubocta ";4;3;1. 1. 1.]       / omnitruncated  (V = |B3| = 48)
census["icosidodecahed";5;3;0. 1. 0.]       / rectified dodecahedron
census["trunc. dodecah";5;3;1. 1. 0.]
census["trunc. icosah.";5;3;0. 1. 1.]       / the football / soccer ball
census["rhombicosidod ";5;3;1. 0. 1.]
census["trunc.icosidod";5;3;1. 1. 1.]       / omnitruncated  (V = |H3| = 120)
census["trunc.tetrahed";3;3;1. 1. 0.]
